# Chapter 9, last part: errors, refused commands, limits, documents with
# equations that are not TeX math, examples.

.h2 Errors, refusals and limits [eq-errors]
.ix Equation errors
.ix Equation messages
.ix Messages, equations
.ix Error, equation
The Editor and the Printer do not look at the same things.  The Editor, which
makes the pictures of the screen, keeps the first message that LaTeX gives.
The text is checked before it is printed, and what fails the check is set as
text.  This section lists every message, the commands and characters that
are refused, and the limits.

.h3 Messages [eq-messages]
.ix Equation, messages of the screen
.ix That TeX command is not allowed in an equation
.ix The equation is empty
.ix latex took more than 5 seconds
.ix The equation did not typeset
.ix Undefined control sequence
.ix Missing }} inserted
.ix Extra }}, or forgotten $
On the screen an equation that fails keeps its label ({c:[EQN text]}); the
message is not drawn in the text.  It is seen in the editor of the equation
with {k:Ctrl-W} ({n:eq-view}), under the line "{c:latex} could not
typeset the equation:".  A message is shown again at once, without
making the picture again, until the text is changed.
.table 28 32
Message | Meaning, and what to do
That TeX command is not allowed in an equation | The text has a command of the
 short list of {x:eq-refused}.  Take the command
 out.
The equation is empty | The text has nothing but spaces and line ends.  Write
 the formula, or delete the marker.
{c:latex} took more than 5 seconds | {c:latex} ran more than 5 seconds and was stopped.  The
 formula is too large or has a loop: make it smaller or simpler.
The equation did not typeset | {c:latex} ended with an error but the log has no
 line that begins with an exclamation mark.  Try the text in smaller parts.
{c:dvipng} did not make an image | LaTeX was satisfied but no picture was made.
 Check that {c:dvipng} works (the prerequisites are in {n:appcmd}).
The message of TeX | What TeX reports about the first thing that it cannot read;
 the table below has the usual ones.
.end
The message of TeX is what TeX reports about the first thing that it cannot
read;
the text of the equation is wrong at or just before the place that the message
names.  These are the messages that the commonest mistakes give, tried with
the text shown.
.table 23 16 19
Message of TeX | Text | Cause
Undefined control sequence. | {c:\badcmd{{x}}} | A command that TeX does not
 have.  Check the spelling in the tables of this chapter.
Missing {{ inserted. | {c:x^} | A command or a script lacks its argument.
Missing }} inserted. | {c:{{a} | A group is not closed.
Extra }}, or forgotten $. | {c:a}}} | A closing brace without an opening one.
Double superscript. | {c:x^2^3} | Two superscripts on one thing; group one:
 {c:x^{{2^3}}}.
Double subscript. | {c:x_1_2} | The same for subscripts.
Misplaced alignment tab character &. | {c:a & b} | The sign & outside an
 environment of columns; write {c:\&} for the sign.
Missing \right. inserted. | {c:\left( x} | A {c:\left} without its {c:\right}.
Extra \right. | {c:\right)} | A {c:\right} without its {c:\left}.
File ended while scanning use of \frac . | {c:\frac{{1}}{{2} | An argument that
 is not closed; the same for {c:\text}.
{c:Please use \mathaccent for accents in math mode.} | {c:\'e} | An accent of text,
 or a letter typed with an accent, in math.
Package amsmath Error: \tag not allowed here. | {c:\tag{{1}}} | A number
 written inside an equation; leave it out.
Package amsmath Error: \begin{{align}} allowed only in paragraph mode. |
 {c:\begin{{align}} a \end{{align}}} | A whole displayed formula of amsmath
 inside an equation; use {c:aligned}.
{c:Package amsmath Error: \begin{{split}} won't work here.} |
 {c:\begin{{split}}...} | The environment split needs a formula of amsmath
 round it; use {c:aligned}.
Misplaced \crcr. | {c:\end{{matrix}}} | An {c:\end} without its {c:\begin}.
.end
A mismatch of delimiters such as {c:\left( x \right]} is not an error: TeX
sets it.  An equation that mixes in words that are not commands (letters side
by side) is not an error either; it is set as variables, in italics
({n:eq-text}).  So a formula that typesets is not necessarily the
formula that was meant: read the picture.

.h3 What the Printer does with a failure [eq-print-fail]
.ix Printer, failed equation
.ix Long form, equations
The Printer never stops for an equation.  An equation that is refused by its
checks, or that TeX cannot set, is printed in the long form: the word EQUATION
and the text, in a small typewriter font, in a box ({n:eq-print}).  The run
goes on and the PDF is made.  The Printer reports the equation as an error,
which is listed in the Error Log ({n:print-errors}).

.h2 The commands that are refused [eq-refused]
.ix Refused commands
.ix Refused equations
.ix Equation, safety
.ix Security, equations
An equation comes from a document, and a document may come from somebody else.
TeX can read and write files and can define commands, so some commands are not
allowed, by two checks of different strength.  The reason is safety: the
text of an equation goes to a program that can open files.  Both checks look
at the name of a command: a backslash and the letters that follow it;
{c:\input} is refused, {c:\inputx} is not on the list and is passed (to TeX,
which does not know it).  The commands that are one backslash and one
other character ({c:\,} {c:\{{} and so on) are never refused.

.h3 The short list of the screen [eq-refused-screen]
.ix Screen check of equations
An equation that has any of these commands is rejected, with the message "That
TeX command is not allowed in an equation".  They are the
commands that open, read and write files, define commands or packages, and
change the category of characters.
.table 63
Commands
input include openin openout read write immediate catcode csname endcsname
 usepackage RequirePackage documentclass newwrite newread closein closeout
 special pdfshellescape scantokens endinput undefined
.end

.h3 The long list of the Printer [eq-refused-print]
.ix Printer check of equations
.ix Equation, long list of refused commands
The Printer applies the list above, and more: all of the following commands,
and any command whose name begins with {c:pdf}, {c:xetex} or {c:luatex}.  A text that has
one of them prints in the long form.
.table 22 38
Group | Commands
Files and the system | input include includeonly InputIfFileExists IfFileExists
 read readline write write18 newwrite newread openin openout closein closeout
 open close immediate special shipout output dump directlua scantokens endinput
 batchmode scrollmode errorstopmode nonstopmode message errmessage typeout url
 href includegraphics verb
Definitions, packages | def gdef edef xdef let futurelet newcommand
 renewcommand providecommand newenvironment renewenvironment
 DeclareRobustCommand DeclareMathOperator usepackage RequirePackage
 documentclass ProvidesPackage NeedsTeXFormat usetikzlibrary tikz pgfmathparse
 ExplSyntaxOn ExplSyntaxOff makeatletter makeatother AtBeginDocument
 AtEndDocument AtBeginShipout mathchardef chardef countdef dimendef toksdef
 skipdef muskipdef newcount newdimen newskip newtoks newif global long outer
 protected
Codes, counters, boxes | catcode lccode uccode sfcode mathcode delcode
 setcounter addtocounter stepcounter refstepcounter setlength addtolength
 settowidth setbox box copy hbox vbox vtop everypar everymath everydisplay
Expansion and control | csname endcsname expandafter noexpand relax
 afterassignment aftergroup begingroup endgroup bgroup egroup loop repeat if
 else fi ifx ifcase ifnum ifdim ifodd or par detokenize fontname font jobname
 meaning string lowercase uppercase romannumeral number the
Page and references | footnote label ref pageref pagestyle thispagestyle
 newpage clearpage cleardoublepage vspace hspace
.end
The commands of that list that a user may miss are {c:\hspace} and
{c:\vspace} (use {c:\quad}, {c:\qquad}, {c:\,}, {c:\phantom} and
{c:\hphantom}, {n:eq-spacing}), {c:\label} and {c:\ref} (there are no
numbers), {c:\footnote}, {c:\font}, {c:\box} and {c:\hbox} (use {c:\text} or
{c:\mathrm}), {c:\number} and {c:\the}, and {c:\if} (an equation is not a
program).  A command of the short list is refused in both places; the others
are accepted by the screen (where TeX determines it) and refused by the Printer,
which is why an equation can show as a picture and still print in the long form.

.h3 Characters and environments [eq-refused-chars]
.ix Equation, refused characters
.ix Equation, allowed environments
.ix Environments of equations
The text is also rejected by the Printer for what it holds apart from commands.
.table 26 34
Text | Refused when
Empty | The text has nothing but spaces, tabs and line ends.
Long | More than 4000 characters.
A character above 127 | Always; the accented letters of the keyboard, the Greek
 letters typed as such, and so on.  Use the commands of the tables.
A control character | Any below the space other than the tab and the line end.
{c:% # $} | Unless written with a backslash: {c:\%}, {c:\#} and {c:\$}.
{c:^^} | Always.
A closing brace | With no opening brace before it.
Unbalanced braces | At the end of the text, when the braces do not match.
{c:\begin \end} | When the environment is not on the list below, or has no
 name in braces.
A backslash at the end | Always: a command that has no name.
.end
The environments that the Printer accepts are these 13; the Editor accepts
any, but TeX can set only some of them in an equation.
.table 17 43
Environment | What it makes
matrix pmatrix bmatrix Bmatrix vmatrix Vmatrix | Matrices without delimiters,
 with parentheses, square brackets, braces, bars and double bars.
smallmatrix | A small matrix for a line of text.
array | A table of columns that have a letter each: c, l or r, and a bar for a
 rule.
cases | A brace with the cases of a definition.
aligned gathered | Lines aligned at the sign &, and lines that are centered.
split | Accepted, but rejected by TeX in an equation ({n:eq-structures}).
subarray | Stacked lines under a limit, as {c:\substack}.
.end
The environments of amsmath that make a whole formula (equation, align, gather,
multline, eqnarray, flalign) are not on the list.  A printed equation that
holds one is set in the long form.

.h2 Limits [eq-limits]
.ix Limits of equations
.ix Equation limits
.ix 4096 pictures
The limits of equations, with what happens at the end of each.
.table 24 36
Limit | Value and what happens
Pictures in one session of the Editor | 4096 different pictures (an equation,
 its mode and its size; the pictures that are enlarged by {k:Ctrl-W} count
 too).  When the limit is reached, an equation that is new keeps its label.
 Leave the Editor and start it again to begin anew.  The printed pages are not
 affected.
Length of an equation on the screen | 1023 characters of the text with the
 lines joined; the rest is cut, and the cut text is what TeX receives.
Length of an equation when printing | 4000 characters ({n:eq-refused-chars}).
Time of a picture | 5 seconds, after which the equation gets the message
 "latex took more than 5 seconds".
Environments when printing | The 13 of {x:eq-refused-chars}.
Lines of the message in the panel | The message of TeX and three lines after it.
.end

.h2 Documents that have equations that are not TeX math [eq-legacy]
.ix Rewriting equations
.ix Equation that is not TeX math
The equations of Strata are TeX math.  A document of the earlier format may
hold equations that are not.  The Printer reports each of them as an error,
prints it as text in the long form, and puts it in the Error Log
({n:print-errors}); the print has then failed, and every page has the banner
and a label such as E1 in front of the equation ({n:print-failed}); on the
screen the equation shows as a label or as a row of letters that is not the
formula.  The document must be rewritten, one equation at a time.  Put the
cursor on the marker and press {k:Alt-F12} or {k:Ctrl-F12}, delete the text,
write the same formula in TeX math with the tables of this chapter
({n:eq-greek} to {n:eq-structures}), press {k:Ctrl-W} to see the result and
{k:Ins} to keep it, and print the document again to check that no equation is
left as text.

.h2 Worked examples [eq-examples]
.ix Examples of equations
.ix Equation examples
Each example gives the text of an equation and its result.  The text is what
is typed in the editor of the equation (a long text may be written on several
lines: a line end is a space, which TeX ignores in math), and the result is
the equation of this document, so it is the typeset form that Strata makes on
the screen and on the printed page.  Every equation is alone in its paragraph,
so it is displayed.  The examples were typeset by the same program as the
tables.

.include equations-x.src
